HTTPS for online content through the AcornSSL module.

OpenTTD downloads online content over HTTPS when it can (libcurl on Unix,
WinHTTP on Windows) and otherwise falls back to a slower route over its own
content-server connection. On RISC OS the new http_riscos.cpp gives it
HTTPS through AcornSSL, the TLS module in RISC OS 5, instead of libcurl:
- a small HTTP/1.1 client (GET/POST, Content-Length, chunked or
  close-delimited bodies, 1xx skipped, up to 5 redirects, 10 s connect and
  30 s idle timeouts) on an "ottd:http" thread, like the curl version;
- AcornSSL with non-blocking calls throughout (a blocking SWI would stop
  every thread and the desktop), the host name set for the certificate
  check and SNI, and no certificate prompt: a bad certificate fails the
  request, and the content download falls back as before;
- if AcornSSL isn't loaded, every request fails straight away and the
  fallback is used.
Tested on Linux with tools/http-test/run.sh (an OpenSSL stand-in for AcornSSL).

For OpenTTD 14.1. See docs/PORTING-NOTES.md in riscos-openttd.

